Handler frame. With one call site per entry point, the compiler inlined the passes and join kernels into the handler, which then realigned its stack on every message. Nothing holding a 256-bit value is inlined into the handler now, and a symbol with at most two orders a side runs scalar passes that need no aligned frame.
(b) on every removal. Every removal on a symbol with an armed order called the (b) pass. An inline price compare (about 4 TSC) now gates it.
Prefetch. It is now forced inline, and the join tactic has one too. The 75 needs your NBBO record prefetched as well; with the tactic’s prefetch alone it is 173.

What I had wrong last time. The bench never armed (b) or passed removal flags, so its default-configuration figures were low, and its prefetch rows measured nothing. Bench parts 11–14 and the new mdsys_2_host_bench.cpp close that.

Costs. on_place is now a call: 60–62 TSC, up from 54–60. With eight armed orders on a side, an execution costs about 6 TSC more (66 → 72).

Compiler. This machine has g++ 13.3, not your 14.2. What gets inlined is the compiler’s choice, so the header now forces it on these paths. The host bench’s header comment has a one-line check to run on your build.

Verification. 720 checks pass in eight build configurations. (a), (b) and the join tactic match brute-force models written from the requirements, and all 151 behaviour-changing mutants are caught.

Yours to decide. Section E.6 of cancel_tactic.pdf lists ten readings where the requirements leave a choice. The four I would look at first:

Shared flags. Two of our orders at one price share the round-lot flags ahead of the first. That still gives a false (b) in 61 of 3,000 streams with several of ours per price. Exact per-order sets need the departing order’s queue handle passed with each removal.
Queue order for (b). A later order of ours counts as at the front only once the earlier one has left the book.
Our own orders in (a). Ours ahead count toward N and K like any other order.
Partly filled, then cancelled. A round lot ahead that trades in part and cancels the rest counts as cancelled.
